const articleHtml: string = "<h1>How to Start Selling Online: A Step-by-Step Guide for Small Businesses</h1>\n<p>Your customers already buy everything else online. The question is whether they can buy from you. For a lot of small businesses, selling online still feels like a big leap: new technology, new costs, new headaches. In reality, a small business can go from no online store to first sale in a few weeks, and you do not need a warehouse, a developer on staff, or a five-figure budget to do it.</p>\n<p>This guide walks through the exact steps, in order, with honest costs and the mistakes that stall new stores before they start.</p>\n<h2>Step 1: Decide What You Will Sell First</h2>\n<p>Do not start by listing everything you carry. Start with your best sellers and the products customers already ask about. Ten to thirty products is plenty for a launch. A focused store is easier to build, easier to photograph well, and easier for customers to browse.</p>\n<p>Write down, in plain words: what you are selling, who it is for, and why they should buy it from you instead of a marketplace giant. Fast local pickup, personal service, and products people cannot get elsewhere are real advantages. Use them in your product descriptions.</p>\n<h2>Step 2: Choose Where You Will Sell</h2>\n<p>You have three real options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Marketplaces (Amazon, eBay, Etsy).</strong> Built-in traffic, but high fees, strict rules, and your customers belong to the platform, not to you. Good for testing a product, weak for building a business.</li>\n<li><strong>Your own online store.</strong> Your brand, your customer list, your rules. This is where long-term value lives, and it is what the rest of this guide covers.</li>\n<li><strong>Social shops (Instagram, Facebook, TikTok).</strong> Useful add-on channels once your own store exists. On their own, they are rented land.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most established small businesses do best with their own store first, then add channels later if the volume justifies it.</p>\n<h2>Step 3: Pick Your E-Commerce Platform</h2>\n<p>For most small businesses the choice comes down to Shopify (simple, hosted, built only for selling) or WooCommerce on WordPress (flexible, no monthly platform fee beyond hosting, better if your site already runs WordPress). Both are proven at scale. Pick based on who will run the store day to day: if nobody on your team wants to touch settings, choose the hosted route.</p>\n<p>Either way, insist on three things: it must look great on phones (most of your traffic will be mobile), it must connect to the payment processor you want, and you must own your domain and your data.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: Set Up Payments, Shipping, and Policies Before You Build Pages</h2>\n<p>These unglamorous pieces decide whether customers finish checkout:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Payments.</strong> Cards plus at least one wallet option (Apple Pay, Google Pay, or PayPal). Every extra step at checkout costs sales.</li>\n<li><strong>Shipping.</strong> Decide flat rate, free over a threshold, local delivery, or in-store pickup. Publish real delivery times, not optimistic ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Policies.</strong> Returns, exchanges, and shipping pages written in plain language. Clear policies do not scare customers off; missing ones do.</li>\n<li><strong>Taxes.</strong> Set up sales tax collection for the states where you have obligations, and ask your accountant about marketplace and remote sales rules before you scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 5: Build Product Pages That Actually Sell</h2>\n<p>A product page has one job: answer every question a customer would ask in your store, then make buying easy.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Photos.</strong> Clear, bright, several angles, and one photo showing scale or the product in use. Phone photos in natural light beat dark professional ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Descriptions.</strong> Lead with the benefit, then the facts: size, material, care, what is in the box. Write like you talk to customers in person.</li>\n<li><strong>Price and availability.</strong> Visible without scrolling, with honest stock status.</li>\n<li><strong>Trust signals.</strong> Reviews when you have them, your return policy summary, and a real way to contact you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 6: Get Your First Traffic</h2>\n<p>A store nobody visits sells nothing, so plan the first traffic before launch, not after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Your existing customers.</strong> Email or text your list, put a sign in the store, add the store link to receipts and invoices. Your first online buyers are usually people who already trust you.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Business Profile.</strong> Add your store link and products. Local searches are the fastest free traffic most small stores ever get.</li>\n<li><strong>Social media.</strong> Post the products in use, not just product shots. Short videos of real use outperform polished ads for small stores.</li>\n<li><strong>One simple offer.</strong> A modest first-order discount or free local pickup gives people a reason to try buying from you this week.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What It Costs to Start Selling Online</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Platform and hosting:</strong> roughly $30 to $100 per month for a hosted store, or hosting plus a one-time build cost for WordPress and WooCommerce.</li>\n<li><strong>Domain:</strong> about $15 per year.</li>\n<li><strong>Payment processing:</strong> typically around 2.9 percent plus 30 cents per transaction. No sale, no fee.</li>\n<li><strong>Professional build (optional but common):</strong> a done-for-you small store usually runs $3,000 to $10,000 depending on product count and integrations. Owners who build it themselves trade that money for 40-plus hours of their own time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Mistakes That Stall New Stores</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Waiting for perfect.</strong> A good store launched this month beats a perfect store launched never. You can improve pages with real orders coming in.</li>\n<li><strong>Listing everything at once.</strong> Two hundred rushed product pages with weak photos sell worse than twenty good ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Hiding shipping costs until checkout.</strong> Surprise fees are the top reason carts get abandoned.</li>\n<li><strong>No plan for traffic.</strong> \"Build it and they will come\" is not a strategy. See Step 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Treating the store as finished.</strong> The stores that grow are the ones whose owners check orders, update products, and add reviews every week.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>\n<p>Start with your best products, your own store, honest policies, and a simple plan for first traffic. That combination gets most small businesses to their first online sales within weeks, and it scales: the same store that sells ten products today can sell five hundred next year.</p>\n<p>That is the path we build on in our <a href=\"https://www.dezignloop.com/services/ecommerce-web-development\">e-commerce development service</a>: your products, your domain, and a store your team can actually run.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Ready to sell online but not sure where to start?</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dezignloop.com/\">Book a free strategy session with DezignLoop</a> and we will map out your store, your costs, and your fastest route to a first sale. No pressure, no jargon. Call us at <strong>+1 (843) 284 3078</strong> or email <strong>support@dezignloop.com</strong>.</p>";

